Document research

Use this method when the person asks for research in the document collections. Match the
number of searches and the depth of reading to the request. A direct question can need no
tool call.

Identify the people, dates, places and records that the question names. Search for relevant
terms and alternate spellings when they can change the result. For a legal question, ask
for a missing jurisdiction if it changes the answer. State which jurisdiction the answer
uses.

Read the source text behind a claim. A search passage can omit context. Compare sources
that have different origins when a claim depends on corroboration. A copied report and its
source give one origin. Treat an explanation as an inference until the records support it.

Use citation handles for documents that support the answer. State the search scope when a
negative answer depends on it. Say which information the collections do not contain when
that limit affects the answer. Present the answer in the form the person requested.
